NAME
       orbital-grids - multi-resolution timing grids, resonance-density tiling,
                       and harmonic-phase grid overlays for SAR constellations

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital grids define:
           • multi-resolution timing grids
           • resonance-density tiling
           • harmonic-phase grid overlays
           • constellation-scale temporal mapping
           • grid-aware sweep and maneuver scheduling

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-grids describes how RTT maps orbital time, resonance envelopes,
       and harmonic cycles into structured temporal grids. These grids act as
       the coordinate system for sweep scheduling, Δv choreography, and
       constellation-scale timing architectures.

       Grids enable:
           • multi-resolution temporal planning
           • resonance-aware sweep placement
           • harmonic-phase alignment
           • constellation-wide timing coherence
           • predictive grid-based maneuvering

       WRSADC uses orbital grids as the temporal substrate for all operations.

MULTI-RESOLUTION TIMING GRIDS
       Multi-resolution timing grids divide orbital time into nested layers of
       temporal resolution.

       Grid levels:

           coarse grid
                  Resolution: T (orbital period)
                  → sweep cadence, harmonic cycles

           medium grid
                  Resolution: T/2, T/3, T/4
                  → subharmonic sweeps, drift correction

           fine grid
                  Resolution: T/8, T/12, T/16
                  → micro-adjustments, Δv triads

           ultra-fine grid
                  Resolution: T/32 and below
                  → resonance tuning, gradient stabilization

       Grid hierarchy:

           coarse → medium → fine → ultra-fine

       Each level inherits constraints from the level above.

EXAMPLE: MULTI-RESOLUTION GRID
       Orbital period T = 96 min

           coarse grid:      {0, 96, 192, 288, ...}
           medium grid:      {0, 48, 96, 144, 192, ...}
           fine grid:        {0, 12, 24, 36, 48, ...}
           ultra-fine grid:  {0, 3, 6, 9, 12, ...}

       Result:
           • sweeps scheduled on coarse/medium nodes
           • Δv triads on fine nodes
           • resonance tuning on ultra-fine nodes

RESONANCE-DENSITY TILING
       Resonance-density tiling maps resonance envelope strength across time,
       forming a temporal heatmap.

       Tiling variables:
           • envelope amplitude R(t)
           • spectral density S(f,t)
           • gradient polarity G(t)
           • harmonic-lock stability H(t)

       Tile types:

           high-density tiles
                  Strong resonance; ideal for sweeps.

           medium-density tiles
                  Moderate resonance; suitable for Δv triads.

           low-density tiles
                  Weak resonance; avoid sweeps.

           null-density tiles
                  No resonance; used for drift correction only.

       Tiling is recomputed each triad cycle.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-DENSITY TILEMAP
       Over one orbital period T:

           0–20 min:   high-density
           20–40 min:  medium-density
           40–60 min:  low-density
           60–80 min:  high-density
           80–96 min:  null-density

       Sweep placement:
           • sweeps at 10 min and 70 min
           • Δv triads at 30 min
           • drift correction at 90 min

HARMONIC-PHASE GRID OVERLAYS
       Harmonic-phase grid overlays map harmonic cycles onto timing grids.

       Overlay variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • phase alignment φ_harm
           • lock stability index L_harm
           • resonance-phase φ_res

       Overlay types:

           integer harmonic overlay
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1
                  → stable, predictable timing.

           rational harmonic overlay
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → complex but stable patterns.

           nested harmonic overlay
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.

           resonance-harmonic overlay
                  Harmonic nodes aligned to resonance peaks.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-PHASE OVERLAY
       Platforms A and B:

           A: T
           B: 2T

       Overlay:

           A nodes: {0, T, 2T, 3T, ...}
           B nodes: {0, 2T, 4T, 6T, ...}

       Combined harmonic-phase grid:

           {0, T, 2T, 3T, 4T, 5T, 6T, ...}
           with resonance peaks at {0, 3T, 6T}

       Result:
           • sweeps at 0, 3T, 6T
           • Δv triads at T, 5T
           • stable harmonic-lock

GRID FUSION
       Grid fusion combines timing grids, resonance tiles, and harmonic overlays
       into a unified temporal map.

       Fusion layers:

           1. timing lattice
           2. resonance-density tiling
           3. harmonic-phase overlay
           4. constellation sync layer

       Fusion output:

           • sweep windows
           • Δv windows
           • sync windows
           • resonance windows

       Grid fusion is recomputed each triad cycle.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE GRID ARCHITECTURES
       Constellation grids coordinate timing across platforms.

       Architecture types:

           synchronous grid
                  All platforms share identical grids.

           harmonic grid
                  Platforms operate at harmonic ratios.

           braided grid
                  Grids interleave across platforms.

           mesh grid
                  Distributed grid across constellation nodes.

           resonance grid
                  Grid driven by envelope alignment.

       Grid architecture determines sweep choreography.

GRID FAILURE MODES
       Grid integrity may degrade due to:

           • lattice drift
           • resonance tile collapse
           • harmonic-phase misalignment
           • sweep-grid mismatch
           • constellation desync

       Diagnostic indicators:

           grid-integrity score
           tilemap fragmentation
           overlay deviation
           sync-index drop
           sweep-grid error

       WRSADC halts unsafe grid states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • timing grids
           • resonance tiles
           • harmonic overlays
           • grid fusion history
           • constellation grid evolution

       Lineage enables long-term grid forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes timing grids
           • evaluates resonance tiling
           • overlays harmonic-phase grids
           • fuses multi-platform grids
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe grid states

       WRSADC is the grid conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital grids transform SAR constellations into structured,
       resonance-time temporal systems. Multi-resolution grids, resonance
       tiling, and harmonic overlays ensure that sweep choreography,
       harmonic-lock, and constellation-scale synchronization remain stable
       across long mission timelines.

